 move with the rook so there's no  way for the knight to cover the uh the  f6 square we have to play rook to f7 and  this is exactly what we wanted because  now uh the rook is preventing the knight  from reaching f7 and we know that knight  to f7 was a very important defending  square  for black in this position so now we  play bishop to f4 threatening bishop to  e5 checkmate and now the knight can no  longer guard the e5 square from f7 so  the knight has to go to c6 to block the  e5 square and now only now we play  bishop to d2  and now bishop to c3 will be checkmate  and that's mating for because now first  the rook was in the way of the knight  from defending against mate now the  knight is in the way of the rogue  because now rook to c7 of course the  knight is in the way bishop to c3 is  just checkmate so that's uh  that's uh that's the idea just uh first  bishop g5 the knight has to uh block  this and oh the rook has to go to f7  block the knight then the knight blocks  the rook and that's how you deliver  checkmate in this position i don't know  the first time i saw this i was very  very impressed and i was very happy with  it and i was more than happy to share  this with my friends at the bar in the  library so maybe maybe you guys would  enjoy that as well so that's the first  one i wanted to show you a very nice  puzzle by paul kerres and now we go to  the second one also very enjoyable it's  more of an attacking one because both  players still have the bishop pair the  queens are on the board two rooks are on  the board but it's um  quite a beautiful attacking pattern that  uh it's a position that can occur in  your own game so maybe just knowing this  will make it a lot easier for you to try  and use it in your own games it's uh  from the 1951 tournament the players are  battered versus  [Music]  i could not find the whole game if i  could i would have shown the game in its  entirety so we're just going to show  this position now it's white to win uh  fe